    {
      "product": "Make Your UNO Kit",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Make Your UNO Kit] [Product category: Kits] [Element of Make Your UNO Kit: None] [Tutorial about Make Your UNO Kit: None] \n\n1.1 Application Examples\nThe Make Your UNO Kit is like any UNO board but created by yourself. The UNO board is the flagship product of Arduino.\nRegardless if you are new to the world of electronics or will use the UNO as a tool for education purposes or industry-related tasks.\nFirst entry to electronics: If this is your first project within coding and electronics, get started with our most used and documented board UNO and create one by yourself using the Make Your UNO Kit is a great choice. It is equipped with the well-known ATmega328P processor, 14 digital input/output pins, 6 analog inputs, USB connections, ICSP header and a reset button. This board includes everything you will need for a great first experience with Arduino.\nEducation purposes: Although the UNO board has been with us for about ten years, it is still widely used for various educational purposes and scientific projects. The Make Your UNO Kit is the perfect way to teach and learn about basic circuits, electronics components, soldering and coding microcontrollers, being the perfect board to create basic and advanced projects of all kinds.\nAudio synthesizer for educational and artistic performances : The kit comes with an Audio Synth Shield that can be attached to the Make Your UNO Kit to learn about and experiment with audio synthesizers, being a perfect device to play and experiment in the classroom and at the same time a great instrument for artistic performances.",
      "product_category": "Kits",
      "chunk_id": "Make Your UNO Kit-datasheet::c4"
    },
    {
      "product": "Due",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Due] [Product category: Mega] [Element of Due: None] [Tutorial about Due: None] \n\n7.5 Board Recovery\nAll Arduino boards have a built-in bootloader which allows flashing the board via USB. In case a sketch locks up the processor and the board is not reachable anymore via USB, it is possible to enter bootloader mode by doubletapping the reset button right after the power-up.",
      "product_category": "Mega",
      "chunk_id": "Due-datasheet::c43"
    },
    {
      "product": "Mega 2560 Rev3",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Mega 2560 Rev3] [Product category: Mega] [Element of Mega 2560 Rev3: None] [Tutorial about Mega 2560 Rev3: None] \n\nDescription\nArduino® Mega 2560 Rev3 is an exemplary development board dedicated for building extensive applications as compared to other maker boards by Arduino. The board accommodates the ATmega2560 microcontroller, which operates at a frequency of 16 MHz. The board contains 54 digital input/output pins, 16 analog inputs, 4 UARTs (hardware serial ports), a USB connection, a power jack, an ICSP header, and a reset button.",
      "product_category": "Mega",
      "chunk_id": "Mega 2560 Rev3-datasheet::c0"
    },
    {
      "product": "Mega 2560 Rev3",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Mega 2560 Rev3] [Product category: Mega] [Element of Mega 2560 Rev3: None] [Tutorial about Mega 2560 Rev3: None] \n\n描述\n与 Arduino 的其他开发板相⽐， Arduino® Mega 2560 Rev3 是⼀款⽤于构建⼴泛应⽤的⽰范性开发板。该电路板内置 ATmega2560 微控制器，⼯作频率为 16 MHz 。电路板包含 54 个数字输⼊ / 输出引脚、 16 个模拟输⼊、 4 个 UART （硬件 串⾏端⼝） 、⼀个 USB 连接、⼀个电源插孔、⼀个 ICSP 接头和⼀个复位按钮。",
      "product_category": "Mega",
      "chunk_id": "Mega 2560 Rev3-datasheet::c44"
    },
    {
      "product": "GIGA R1 WiFi",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: GIGA R1 WiFi] [Product category: Mega] [Element of GIGA R1 WiFi: None] [Tutorial about GIGA R1 WiFi: Tutorial_GIGA R1] \n\n1. Download the GIGAR1_MP_FW.dfu firmware (latest firmware is always available at the MicroPython with Arduino page). 2. Download the dfu-util tool, or managers). Make sure it is in your PATH. S brew install dfu-util 3. Double tap the reset button on the GIGA R1 board. The green LED should now be pulsing. This means it is in bootloader mode.",
      "product_category": "Mega",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_GIGA R1_MicroPython::c4"
    },
    {
      "product": "GIGA R1 WiFi",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: GIGA R1 WiFi] [Product category: Mega] [Element of GIGA R1 WiFi: None] [Tutorial about GIGA R1 WiFi: Tutorial_GIGA R1] \n\n3. Open a terminal, and navigate to the directory of your GIGAR1_MP_FW.dfu_ file. Run the following command: § dfu-util -w -a @ -d 2 At the end of the process, you will see the following message on success: Done parsing DfuSe file 5. Reset the board by clicking the reset button (just once). You should now be able to view and access some of the GIGA R1 boards onboard storage as a mass storage device in your file manager. Congratulations! You have now installed MicroPython on the GIGA R1 board. Please note that after this installation is complete, your board will no longer be detected as an Arduino device. In order to go back e to using the Arduino programming environment, you need to reset the board. This is simply done by double tapping the reset button.",
      "product_category": "Mega",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_GIGA R1_MicroPython::c5"
    },
    {
      "product": "GIGA R1 WiFi",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: GIGA R1 WiFi] [Product category: Mega] [Element of GIGA R1 WiFi: None] [Tutorial about GIGA R1 WiFi: Tutorial_GIGA R1] \n\nKeyboard Important! When using the GIGA as a keyboard, make sure to include some sort of delay. Otherwise, you may end up printing things very e fast, which can be an annoyance. If this happens nonetheless, double tap the reset button and upload a blank sketch to reset the board. To emulate a keyboard, we need to include PluggableUSBHID.h and USBKeyboard.h , and create an object using the USBkeyboard constructor. #include \"PluggableUSBH #inelude \"USBKeyboard.h USBKeyboard Keyboard; ns, To send a single character, we can use the putc() method. Keyboard.putc(97); //pr See the DEC column at asciicode.com to understand what number you need to print a specific character. To print a whole string, use the printf() method. Keyboard.printf (\"Hello To use modifiers and function keys, use the key_code() method.",
      "product_category": "Mega",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_GIGA R1_USB Features::c14"
    },
