    {
      "product": "Make Your UNO Kit",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Make Your UNO Kit] [Product category: Kits] [Element of Make Your UNO Kit: None] [Tutorial about Make Your UNO Kit: None] \n\n3.2 Processor\nThe Main Processor is an ATmega328P running at up to 20 MHz. Most of its pins are connected to the external headers, however, some are reserved for internal communication with the USB Bridge coprocessor ( ATMega16U2 ) inside the USB-C® Serial PCB Board .",
      "product_category": "Kits",
      "chunk_id": "Make Your UNO Kit-datasheet::c10"
    },
    {
      "product": "Make Your UNO Kit",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Make Your UNO Kit] [Product category: Kits] [Element of Make Your UNO Kit: None] [Tutorial about Make Your UNO Kit: None] \n\n1.1 Application Examples\nThe Make Your UNO Kit is like any UNO board but created by yourself. The UNO board is the flagship product of Arduino.\nRegardless if you are new to the world of electronics or will use the UNO as a tool for education purposes or industry-related tasks.\nFirst entry to electronics: If this is your first project within coding and electronics, get started with our most used and documented board UNO and create one by yourself using the Make Your UNO Kit is a great choice. It is equipped with the well-known ATmega328P processor, 14 digital input/output pins, 6 analog inputs, USB connections, ICSP header and a reset button. This board includes everything you will need for a great first experience with Arduino.\nEducation purposes: Although the UNO board has been with us for about ten years, it is still widely used for various educational purposes and scientific projects. The Make Your UNO Kit is the perfect way to teach and learn about basic circuits, electronics components, soldering and coding microcontrollers, being the perfect board to create basic and advanced projects of all kinds.\nAudio synthesizer for educational and artistic performances : The kit comes with an Audio Synth Shield that can be attached to the Make Your UNO Kit to learn about and experiment with audio synthesizers, being a perfect device to play and experiment in the classroom and at the same time a great instrument for artistic performances.",
      "product_category": "Kits",
      "chunk_id": "Make Your UNO Kit-datasheet::c4"
    },
    {
      "product": "Make Your UNO Kit",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Make Your UNO Kit] [Product category: Kits] [Element of Make Your UNO Kit: None] [Tutorial about Make Your UNO Kit: None] \n\n3.1.1 Board Topology And Bill Of Materials\nMake Your UNO components showcase\nThe BOM or Bill Of Materials of the Make Your UNO Kit is made up of common components that can be found on the market. Use this list to reference and/or replace a damaged component if necessary. The designator references can be found written on the board and inside the board source files.\nMake Your UNO board topology\nC3, C8, C9, C10, C11, C12, C13, Quantity = 7. C3, C8, C9, C10, C11, C12, C13, Description = Capacitor THD 100nF 63V. C1, C4, C5, Quantity = 3. C1, C4, C5, Description = Capacitor THD 22pF 50V C0G 5%. C2, C6, C7, Quantity = 3. C2, C6, C7, Description = Capacitor ALUM POL THD 47µF 25V. D1, Quantity = 1. D1, Description = Rectifier diode [1N4007]. J1, Quantity = 1. J1, Description = UNO header connector - analog I/Os 14 positions. J2, Quantity = 1. J2, Description = UNO header connector - digital I/Os 18 positions. J3, Quantity = 1. J3, Description = SPI header. J4, Quantity = 1. J4, Description = Power supply jack, 6 position. J5, Quantity = 1. J5, Description = USB-C® to Serial breakout board. J7, Quantity = 1. J7, Description = IC socket connector dual in place 28 positions. LD1, LD2, LD3, LD4, Quantity = 4. LD1, LD2, LD3, LD4, Description = LED Yellow diffused,3mm. LD5, Quantity = 1. LD5, Description = LED Green diffused,3mm. PB1, Quantity = 1. PB1, Description = Push-Button. Q1, Quantity = 1. Q1, Description = N-Channel Mosfet Transistor 0.2A. R1, R4, R5, R6, R7, R8, R11, Quantity = 7. R1, R4, R5, R6, R7, R8, R11, Description = Axial resistor 1kΩ, 1/4W, 5%. R2, R9, Quantity = 2. R2, R9, Description = Axial resistor 10kΩ, 1/4W, 5%. R3, R10, Quantity = 2. R3, R10, Description = Axial resistor 1MΩ, 1/4w, 5%. U2, Quantity = 1. U2, Description = LDO Regulator 3.3V. Y1, Quantity = 1. Y1, Description = Quartz crystal oscillator 16MHz 20pF THD. , Quantity = 1. , Description = PCB Arduino Make Your UNO. , Quantity = 1. , Description = Jumper wire Red 15cm. , Quantity = 1. , Description = Jumper wire Black 15cm\nSome of the components listed above are available in a replacement pack on the Arduino Store.",
      "product_category": "Kits",
      "chunk_id": "Make Your UNO Kit-datasheet::c9"
    }
