  "chunks": [
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Family] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: None] \n\nDescription\nThe Arduino MKR Vidor 4000 (from now on referred to as MKR Vidor 4000) is without a doubt the most advanced and featured-packed board in the MKR family and the only one with a FPGA chip on board. With a camera & HDMI connector, a Wi-Fi® / Bluetooth® module and up to 25 configurable pins, the board offers a huge range of possibilities to implement solutions in different environments and applications.",
      "product_category": "MKR Family",
      "chunk_id": "MKR Vidor 4000-datasheet::c0"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Family] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: None] \n\n1 Features\nThe MKR Vidor 4000 is nothing less than a powerhouse of a board, packing a huge set of features into a small form factor. It features the Intel® Cyclone® 10CL016 for FPGA (Field Programming Gate Array), allowing you to configure a large set of pins to accommodate any of your preferences. But why stop there? The board also has a camera connector, a Micro HDMI connector, Wi-Fi® / Bluetooth® connectivity through the NINA-W102 module, and cybersecurity through the ECC508 crypto chip. Just like the other members of the MKR family, it uses the popular Arm® Cortex®-M0 32-bit SAMD21 microprocessor.",
      "product_category": "MKR Family",
      "chunk_id": "MKR Vidor 4000-datasheet::c3"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Family] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: None] \n\n1.2 FPGA Block\nThe FPGA is the Intel® Cyclone® 10CL016. It contains 16K logic elements, 504 kB of embedded RAM, and x56 18x18 bits HW multipliers for high-speed DSP operations. Each pin can toggle at over 150 MHz and can be configured for functions such as UARTs, (Q)SPI, high-resolution/high-frequency PWM, quadrature encoder, I2C, I2S, Sigma Delta DAC, etc.\nFPGA Intel® Cyclone® 10CL016, Details = . Mini PCI Express port with programmable pins, Details = PCI. MIPI camera connector, Details = Camera Connector. Micro HDMI, Details = Video Output. , Details = Circuit Operating Voltage 3.3 V. 22 headers + 25 Mini PCI Express, Details = Digital I/O Pins. All Pins, Details = PWMPins. Up to 7 (depends on FPGA configuration), Details = UART. Up to 7 (depends on FPGA configuration), Details = SPI. Up to 7 (depends on FPGA configuration), Details = I2C. 4 or 8 mA, Details = DC Current per I/O Pin. 2 MB, Details = Flash Memory. 8 MB, Details = SDRAM. Clock Speed, Details = 48 MHz - up to 200 MHz\nThe board comes with 8 MB of SRAM to support the FPGA operations on video and audio. The FPGA code is stored in a 2 MB QSPI Flash chip, of which 1 MB is allocated for user applications. It is possible to perform high-speed DSP operations for audio and video processing. Therefore, the Vidor includes a Micro HDMI connector for audio and video output and a MIPI camera connector for video input. All of the board's pins are driven both by SAMD21 and FPGA while respecting the MKR family format. Finally, there is a Mini PCI Express connector with up to x25 userprogrammable pins that can be used for connecting your FPGA as a peripheral to a computer or to create your own PCI interfaces.",
      "product_category": "MKR Family",
      "chunk_id": "MKR Vidor 4000-datasheet::c5"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Family] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: None] \n\n3 Functional Overview\nThe cores of the MKR Vidor 4000 are the SAMD21 Arm® Cortex®-M0+ microcontroller and the Intel® Cyclone® 10CL016 FPGA. The board also contains several peripherals connected to the microcontroller and the FPGA blocks.",
      "product_category": "MKR Family",
      "chunk_id": "MKR Vidor 4000-datasheet::c10"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Familiy] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000] \n\n< Go Back Hardware MKR Vidor 4000 v Tutorials Draw Arduino Logo with MkR Vidor 4000 Enable Camera on MKR Vidor 4000 Encoder Example With MKR Vidor 4000 Getting Started With FPGAs Using the MKR Vidor 4000 Host a Web Server on the MKR Vidor 4000 QR Code Recognition Using the MKR Vidor 4000 with Quaratus IDE Search on Docs Home / Hardware / MKR Vidor 4000 / Getting Started With FPGAs Using the MKR Vidor 4000 This tutorial refers to a product that has reached its end-of-life status. Getting Started With FPGAs Using the MKR Vidor 4000 Learn and understand the basics of how FPGA and HDL works. Daniel Last Hertz revision » 12/19/2023 Tutorial by Daniel Hertz, originally published on Maker.pro Intimidated by FPGAs? Don't be!",
      "product_category": "MKR Familiy",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000_Getting Started With FPGAs::c0"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Familiy] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000] \n\nLearn about Arduino's most programmable board with this Arduino MKR Vidor 4000 tutorial. The Arduino MKR Vidor 4000 is an interesting Arduino development board for many reasons. It not only contains a 32-Bit MCU, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth®, a camera interface, and built-in HDMI support but it also features an on-board Intel Cyclone 10 FPGA. usB RESET 7m on: . LiPo connector SAMD21 FPGA MEMORY What Is an FPGA? Setting Up the Basics The Vidor 4000 and Verilog Compiling and Uploading Code Compile the FPGA Code Upload the Sketch and FPGA Configuration Final Reminders About the Vidor 4000 MKR Vidor 4000 mapping. What Is an FPGA? A field-programmable gate array (FPGA) is an integrated circuit that can be freely configured by a user again and again to implement a logic function. This process is similar to programming a microcontroller. However, on an FPGA, you don't upload software that runs on set hardware.",
      "product_category": "MKR Familiy",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000_Getting Started With FPGAs::c1"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Familiy] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000] \n\nInstead, you configure the FPGA to be the hardware itself. This enables you to implement very fast filters, audio and video processors, and other things that microcontrollers aren't able to do. You can think of the Intel Cyclone on the MkR Vidor as a freely configurable expansion board that allows you to implement different hardware that can be used with the Arduino. If you're interested in a deeper dive around FPGAs, check out his article about FPGA HDL basics. Setting Up the Basics Before you can begin, you need are using Windows, the necessary drivers will be installed automatically when you perform these steps. In Arduino IDE, select Tools and open the board manager; in the window that opens up, search for \"MKR 4000\" and install the following package:\nBoard Manager for the MKR Vidor 4000 in the IDE. Once that's done, you are ready to go!",
      "product_category": "MKR Familiy",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000_Getting Started With FPGAs::c2"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Familiy] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000] \n\nARDUINODOCS Q _ Search on Docs / < Go Back Home / Hardware / MKR Vidor ON THIS PAGE Hardware 4000 / Using the MKR Vidor 4000 < with Quaratus IDE Quartus Installation and First Launch MKR Vidor 4000 v This tutorial refers to a User Interface Basics Tutorials product that has How to Set Up a New Project reached its end-of-life I/O Pin Assignment Draw Arduino Logo with status. MKR Vidor 4000 Loading and Creating Files Enable Camera on MKR How to Start the Compilation Vidor 4000 Using the M KR Process H H Uploading the Code Encoder Example With MKR Vidor 4000 with Vidor 4000 Vidor 4000's FPGA Is Ready to Quaratus IDE Go! Getting Started With FPGAs Using the MKR Vidor 4000 Learn how to program the FPGA with the Quartus IDE.",
      "product_category": "MKR Familiy",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000_with Quaratus IDE::c0"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Familiy] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000] \n\nHost a Web Server on the MKR Vidor 4000 Daniel Last . Hertz revision QR Code Recognition Using the MKR Vidor 4000 with Quaratus IDE Tutorial by Daniel Hertz, originally published on Maker.pro We covered how to get started with the Arduino MKR Vidor 4000, showing you how to configure the FPGA on the board. For that purpose, we used a pre-compiled file. In this article, we will introduce you to the basics of working with the Quartus, the default IDE that comes with the FPGA and show you the necessary steps to create a basic Verilog project that can be compiled and uploaded to the Arduino. Quartus Installation and First Launch During the set-up process, you'll be asked to install support files for different Intel FPGAs. However, don't install any boards for now. Instead, navigate to Intel's download page and search for the FPGA family you want to use. Make sure to select the same version of Quartus that you installed on your computer:",
      "product_category": "MKR Familiy",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000_with Quaratus IDE::c1"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Familiy] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000] \n\nIPs (Intellectual Properties) are pre-built blocks for common recurring tasks, like communication protocols and signal generation. Main work area (middle of the screen) How to Set Up a New Proiect Launch the project wizard from the main menu-bar. Select New Project Wizard in Quartus IDE. The assistant will take you through the process step by step. On the first screen, simply click Next. Then, enter your project's name and the name of the top-level design entity. Enter your project's name and top-level entity. On the third screen, choose Empty Project and click next. Skip the next screen, as we will add files to the project later. The fifth pane requires you to select the FPGA you're using. In my case, it's the 10CL016YU256C8G. udm tem btn mi Select the FPGA you're using. The exact model number is printed on the chip. Skip the sixth screen for now.",
      "product_category": "MKR Familiy",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000_with Quaratus IDE::c4"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Family] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: None] \n\n2.1 Recommended Operating Conditions\nThe following table is a comprehensive guideline for the optimal use of the MKR Vidor 4000, outlining typical operating conditions and design limits. The operating conditions of the MKR Vidor 4000 are largely a function based on its component's specifications.\nUSB Supply Input Voltage, Min = -. USB Supply Input Voltage, Typ = 5.0. USB Supply Input Voltage, Max = -. USB Supply Input Voltage, Unit = V. Battery Supply Input Voltage, Min = -. Battery Supply Input Voltage, Typ = 3.7. Battery Supply Input Voltage, Max = -. Battery Supply Input Voltage, Unit = V. Supply Input Voltage, Min = -. Supply Input Voltage, Typ = 5.0. Supply Input Voltage, Max = 6.0. Supply Input Voltage, Unit = V. Microprocessor Circuit Operating Voltage, Min = -. Microprocessor Circuit Operating Voltage, Typ = 3.3. Microprocessor Circuit Operating Voltage, Max = -. Microprocessor Circuit Operating Voltage, Unit = V. FPGA Circuit Operating Voltage, Min = -. FPGA Circuit Operating Voltage, Typ = 3.3. FPGA Circuit Operating Voltage, Max = -. FPGA Circuit Operating Voltage, Unit = V",
      "product_category": "MKR Family",
      "chunk_id": "MKR Vidor 4000-datasheet::c9"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Family] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: None] \n\n3.1 Pinout\nThe basic pinout is shown in Figure 1.\n\nThe pinout of the main FPGA connections is shown in Figure 2.\n\nCheck the official Arduino documentation to see the full pinout document and the schematics of the product.",
      "product_category": "MKR Family",
      "chunk_id": "MKR Vidor 4000-datasheet::c11"
    }
  ]
