    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Familiy] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000] \n\nHost a Web Server on the MKR Vidor 4000 Daniel Last . Hertz revision QR Code Recognition Using the MKR Vidor 4000 with Quaratus IDE Tutorial by Daniel Hertz, originally published on Maker.pro We covered how to get started with the Arduino MKR Vidor 4000, showing you how to configure the FPGA on the board. For that purpose, we used a pre-compiled file. In this article, we will introduce you to the basics of working with the Quartus, the default IDE that comes with the FPGA and show you the necessary steps to create a basic Verilog project that can be compiled and uploaded to the Arduino. Quartus Installation and First Launch During the set-up process, you'll be asked to install support files for different Intel FPGAs. However, don't install any boards for now. Instead, navigate to Intel's download page and search for the FPGA family you want to use. Make sure to select the same version of Quartus that you installed on your computer:",
      "product_category": "MKR Familiy",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000_with Quaratus IDE::c1"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Familiy] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000] \n\nIPs (Intellectual Properties) are pre-built blocks for common recurring tasks, like communication protocols and signal generation. Main work area (middle of the screen) How to Set Up a New Proiect Launch the project wizard from the main menu-bar. Select New Project Wizard in Quartus IDE. The assistant will take you through the process step by step. On the first screen, simply click Next. Then, enter your project's name and the name of the top-level design entity. Enter your project's name and top-level entity. On the third screen, choose Empty Project and click next. Skip the next screen, as we will add files to the project later. The fifth pane requires you to select the FPGA you're using. In my case, it's the 10CL016YU256C8G. udm tem btn mi Select the FPGA you're using. The exact model number is printed on the chip. Skip the sixth screen for now.",
