      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: GIGA Display Shield] [Product category: Mega] [Element of GIGA Display Shield: None] [Tutorial about GIGA Display Shield: None] \n\n6.2 Back View\nBack View of Arduino GIGA Display Shield\nU2, Description = IS31FL3197-QFLS2-TR. U2, Ref. = U3. U2, Description = LV52204MTTBG LED Driver IC. U7, Description = BMI270 6-axis IMU. U7, Ref. = J3. U7, Description = 21TW-247 Camera Connector 2.54 mmHeader. J4, Description = F32Q-1A7H1-11020 Display Connector. J4, Ref. = J5. J4, Description = F32Q-1A7H1-11008 Touch Connector. J6, Description = 2.54 mmHeader GIGA Connector. J6, Ref. = J7. J6, Description = 2.54mm Header GIGA Connector",
      "product_category": "Mega",
      "chunk_id": "GIGA Display Shield-datasheet::c9"
    },
    {
      "product": "GIGA Display Shield",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: GIGA Display Shield] [Product category: Mega] [Element of GIGA Display Shield: None] [Tutorial about GIGA Display Shield: None] \n\n8 6-Axis IMU\nThe GIGA Display Shield provides 6-axis IMU capabilities, via the 6-axis BMI270 (U7) IMU. The BMI270 includes both a three-axis gyroscope as well as a three-axis accelerometer. The information obtained can be used for measuring raw movement parameters as well as for machine learning. The BMI270 is connected to the GIGA R1 WiFi via a common I2C connection.",
      "product_category": "Mega",
      "chunk_id": "GIGA Display Shield-datasheet::c11"
    },
    {
      "product": "Braccio Carrier",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Braccio Carrier] [Product category: Education] [Element of Braccio Carrier: None] [Tutorial about Braccio Carrier: None] \n\nSYSTEMBLOCKDIAGRAM\nArduino Braccio Carrier System Block Diagram\nThe USB powers the Arduino® Nano RP2040 Connect which is the heart of the Braccio Carrier as it stores the programs responsible for the functioning of the whole system. The Nano is connected to the joystick which is the input peripheral and LED display screen which is the output peripheral of the microcontroller. Another power supply via USB-C® powers the connected motors which constitute the entire robotics functionality the system. RS485 Transceiver signals the motor connector for the precise motihe motors according to the input given by the user.\nArduino Braccio Carrier Block Diagram",
      "product_category": "Education",
      "chunk_id": "Braccio Carrier-datasheet::c17"
    },
    {
      "product": "MKR Vidor 4000",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: MKR Vidor 4000] [Product category: MKR Familiy] [Element of MKR Vidor 4000: None] [Tutorial about MKR Vidor 4000: Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000] \n\n< Go Back Tutorials Search on Docs Home / Tutorials / WiFiNINA Library Examples WiFiNINA Library Examples The WiFi library for boards with a NINA WiFi module. Works with the MKR WiFi 1010, MKR Vidor 4000, Uno WiFi Rev2, Nano 33 loT and the Nano RP2040 Connect. Examples NINA Firmware Code Hardware Required Circuit WiFININA AP Simple Web Server WIFININA Connect No Encryption WifiNINA Connect With WEP WifiNINA Connect With WPA WifiNINA Scan Networks WifiNINA Scan Networks Advanced WifiNINA Simple Web Server WiFi WifiNINA Udp NTP Client WifiNINA WiFi Chat Server WifiNINA WiFi Ping WifiNINA WiFi SSL Client WifiNINA WiFi Udp Send Receive String WifiNINA WiFi Web Client WifiNINA WiFi Web Client Repeating WifiNINA WiFi Web Server Firmware Version Firmware Update The WiFiNINA library is designed for Arduino boards using a NINA W-10 series module.",
      "product_category": "MKR Familiy",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_MKR Vidor 4000_WiFiNINA Library::c0"
    },
    {
      "product": "Nano RP2040 Connect",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Nano RP2040 Connect] [Product category: Nano Family] [Element of Nano RP2040 Connect: None] [Tutorial about Nano RP2040 Connect: Tutorial_Nano RP2040 Connect] \n\n(download) 2. Unzip the contents, and locate the firmware.uf2 file inside of the ARDUINO_NANO_RP2040_CONN ECT folder. 3. Force the bootloader on the Nano RP2040 Connect, by connecting a jumper wire between GND and REC pins as shown in the image below. When the mass storage device opens, drag the firmware.uf2 onto it, and the latest version will install. Connect GND + REC + press the reset button to open mass storage. 4. Install and open the Thonny IDE. Navigate to Run > Select Interpreter and choose the \"MicroPython(generic)\" from the list. Your board should now appear in the other dropdown menu:\nThonny board/port selection. If your board appears, it has been successful.",
      "product_category": "Nano Family",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_Nano RP2040 Connect_Datalogger with MicroPython::c3"
    },
    {
      "product": "Nano RP2040 Connect",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Nano RP2040 Connect] [Product category: Nano Family] [Element of Nano RP2040 Connect: None] [Tutorial about Nano RP2040 Connect: Tutorial_Nano RP2040 Connect] \n\nPrint motion activity in the Serial Terminal of the OpenMV IDE. Hardware and Software Needed Arduino Nano RP2040 Connect board. OpenMV IDE. Micro-USB cable (x1). Inertial Measurement Unit An Inertial Measurement Unit gravity and angular rate. Typically, an IMU consists of three devices:\nGyroscope: a device that measures the system's angular rate. Accelerometer: a device that measures the system's specific force/acceleration. Magnetometer: an optional device that measures the surrounding magnetic field of the system. IMU's are typically used to capture movement data of a device or a system. The onboard IMU of the Nano RP2040 Connect, the LSM6DSOX from STMicroelectronics®, has an embedded hardware processing engine dedicated to real-time machine learning computing; this means that some machine learning algorithms were moved from the application processor to the LSM6DSOxX directly. STMicroelectronics named this embedded processing engine of the LSM6DSOX Machine Learning Core (MLC). The LSM6DSOX Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU).",
      "product_category": "Nano Family",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_Nano RP2040 Connect_MicroPython and the IMU Machine Learning::c3"
    },
    {
      "product": "Nano RP2040 Connect",
      "file": "docling_chunks.jsonl",
      "text": "[Product: Nano RP2040 Connect] [Product category: Nano Family] [Element of Nano RP2040 Connect: None] [Tutorial about Nano RP2040 Connect: Tutorial_Nano RP2040 Connect] \n\nIn the MLC, machine learning processing is implemented tool composed of a series of configurable nodes; each node is characterized by an \"if-thenelse\" condition where an input signal (represented by statistical parameters calculated from the sensor data) is evaluated against a certain threshold. The decision tree results can be read from the application processor at any time and there is also the possibility to generate an interrupt for every change in the result in the decision tree. Using the MLC for Vibration Monitoring We will use the LSM6DSOX accelerometer from our Nano RP2040 Connect board, its MLC, and a pre-trained model to implement a simple vibration monitoring script. With this script, and the pre-trained model, three types of vibrations can be detected:\n1 Novibration. 2 Lowvibration. 3 High vibration. Circuit Dhiia in vwatir Nanna board to your computer.",
      "product_category": "Nano Family",
      "chunk_id": "Tutorial_Nano RP2040 Connect_MicroPython and the IMU Machine Learning::c4"
    },
    {
      "product": "Braccio Carrier",
